## OpenInference changes Anthropic stream records and preserves parallel tool replies

Source: Arize AI / OpenInference — https://github.com/Arize-ai/openinference/releases/tag/%40arizeai/openinference-instrumentation-anthropic%401.0.0
Published: 2026-10-23
Channel: github

OpenInference’s JavaScript Anthropic integration records model requests and tool activity for debugging AI applications. Version 1.0.0 supports the current stable and beta Messages API with Anthropic SDK 0.133, and changes the shape of some recorded data. A streamed response is now stored as a reconstructed JSON message rather than concatenated plain text. When a system prompt is present, it becomes input message zero; the remaining conversation starts at index one.

The release also fixes a loss of evidence when an input message contains several tool results. Previously, only the last result survived in the trace. Each result is now recorded as its own tool message. For illustration, an assistant receiving inventory and shipping replies together should leave both replies available for investigation, rather than an apparently incomplete exchange.

Streams are observed through the caller’s iterator, and the notes describe separate completion, early-exit and error outcomes. The system prompt also moves out of invocation parameters, where hideInputs did not conceal it. Teams consuming trace exports should check JSON parsing, message indexes and masking behavior before adopting this major instrumentation version.

Why it matters: Anthropic trace consumers should update assumptions about output formats and message positions, then check multi-tool and masked-input examples.

