## Langfuse documents enforced FIPS mode for self-hosted Enterprise deployments

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-23-fips-mode
Published: 2026-10-23
Channel: news

Langfuse stores traces and evaluation records from AI applications, so its deployment becomes part of a team’s data-protection boundary. An October 23 changelog explains FIPS mode for Self-Hosted Enterprise installations: cryptographic operations use validated modules in approved mode, and startup fails when required conditions are missing. This concerns validated cryptographic components; the announcement explicitly says Langfuse itself is not FIPS validated.

Administrators enable the mode with LANGFUSE_REQUIRE_FIPS=true on both web and worker containers. Hosts must also run in FIPS mode. The documented startup checks reject a missing Enterprise license, an unsuitable host or a storage option requiring MD5. For illustration, a government deployment can give an assessor the module certificate numbers and enforcement mechanism rather than treating a container image alone as proof of compliance.

The feature is available from version 4.57.0, rather than being a newly required server upgrade. Hardened images omit package managers, curl and Python; the article recommends wget for health checks and multi-stage builds for extensions. Existing password users must reset passwords after FIPS mode is enabled, and transactional email needs configuring first. These operational prerequisites deserve testing before a rollout.

Why it matters: Teams with validated-cryptography requirements should test host mode, storage compatibility, health checks and password recovery before enabling enforcement.

