## Weave removes old imports that can stop saved operations loading

Source: Weights & Biases / Weave — https://github.com/wandb/weave/releases/tag/v0.54.0
Published: 2026-10-23
Channel: github

Weave records AI application operations so teams can inspect and evaluate their behavior. Its Python release 0.54.0 removes the weave.shared and weave.trace_server alias modules. Code importing either path now raises ModuleNotFoundError. The preceding 0.53.13 release issued FutureWarning messages for those imports, so this update turns an earlier warning into an enforced compatibility change.

The release identifies a second consequence beyond application startup: a saved operation whose code imports from weave.shared loads as None in this version. For illustration, an evaluation workflow that retrieves a previously saved operation could lose the callable it expects, even if its current application entry point no longer imports the old module. Checking only whether the main service starts would miss that case.

The notes separately deprecate weave.trace_server_bindings.stainless_remote_http_trace_server and direct users to import RemoteHTTPTraceServer from weave.trace_server_bindings.remote_http_trace_server. They do not provide a blanket replacement for every removed alias. Before upgrading, teams should inventory old imports and test loading representative saved operations, using the release’s linked change for the migration details relevant to their code.

Why it matters: Weave maintainers should check application imports and stored operation code before upgrading, and adopt the documented RemoteHTTPTraceServer import where applicable.

